# Agent-steering files are executable policy for every AI agent working in
# this repository (issue #374, F3): a single merged edit to any of them
# persistently changes what those agents do on every future task, so edits
# here must never land without maintainer review. Keep this list in sync with
# tests/bats/agent_docs_codeowners.bats, which fails when a steering path
# loses coverage.

/CLAUDE.md @Kravalg
/AGENTS.md @Kravalg
/cursor-project-guide.md @Kravalg
/.claude/ @Kravalg
/scripts/get-pr-comments.sh @Kravalg
/.github/CODEOWNERS @Kravalg

# --- Approved-by-definition artefacts and the gates themselves (issue #344) ---
#
# Three classes of file where a merged mistake stops any automated check from
# ever catching it again, so the only remaining control is a human reading the
# diff. The team slugs the issue proposed (@VilnaCRM-Org/design-reviewers,
# ci-owners, infra-owners) do not exist — `gh api orgs/VilnaCRM-Org/teams`
# returns an empty list — so coverage is assigned to the maintainer, exactly as
# the agent-steering block above does. Swap in the teams when they exist.
#
# NOTE: CODEOWNERS alone only auto-REQUESTS review. Making it blocking needs
# "Require review from Code Owners" on the `main` ruleset. That rule is committed
# in config/main-ruleset.json (`require_code_owner_review: true`, issue #343), but
# a ruleset is a repository setting: it takes effect only once an admin applies it
# with scripts/ci/apply-branch-ruleset.sh — see CONTRIBUTING.md, "The `main`
# ruleset (issue #343)".

# Visual baselines. Once a wrong baseline is approved, the visual gate passes BY
# DEFINITION and can never catch that regression again: #242 (2025-10-06) shipped
# incorrect swagger HTTP-method badges and the fix had to rewrite ~30 committed
# PNGs. The `**` form already covers both depths the snapshot directories sit at
# (`src/test/visual/*.spec.ts-snapshots/` and `src/test/visual/swagger/…`); the
# explicit depth-1 line is kept beside it so the common case is visible to a
# reader, and because these paths are worth over- rather than under-matching.
/src/test/visual/*-snapshots/ @Kravalg
/src/test/visual/**/*-snapshots/ @Kravalg

# Workflows. autorelease.yml, deploy.yml and the sandbox pair carry
# `contents: write`-class privileges and assume AWS roles, and every `uses:` here
# is a supply-chain edge.
/.github/workflows/ @Kravalg

# The CloudFront edge. scripts/cloudfront_routing.js is the most-fixed file in the
# repository (four production incidents: #226, #229, #235/#248, #249/#250) and it
# runs at the edge, where the static export cannot honour Next `headers()` — so it
# is also the only enforcement point for the security-header policy.
/scripts/cloudfront_routing.js @Kravalg
/scripts/cloudfront_security_headers.js @Kravalg

# The gate configs. Weakening one of these is the quietest way to make a red
# check green: TypeScript strictness, an ESLint rule, the Jest coverage
# thresholds, the mutation and metrics budgets, the import boundaries, the
# security-header baseline. config/ is owned as a directory because every file in
# it is a gate policy, so a new one is covered the day it lands.
# scripts/ci/ is the other half of the same class: the code that ENFORCES those
# policies. Editing `-lt 60` in check-security-txt.sh is a quieter way to turn a
# red check green than editing config/ is, and CLAUDE.md forbids exactly that.
/scripts/ci/ @Kravalg

/tsconfig.json @Kravalg
/eslint.config.mjs @Kravalg
/jest.config.ts @Kravalg
/stryker.config.mjs @Kravalg
/playwright.config.ts @Kravalg
/.dependency-cruiser.js @Kravalg
/config/ @Kravalg

# --- The invocation surface of every gate (issue #337) ---
#
# config/ and scripts/ci/ above own what a gate CHECKS; these own what INVOKES
# it. A gate that is never run is as absent as one whose threshold was lowered,
# and the line that runs it is the quieter of the two places to weaken it: a
# Makefile recipe that drops a flag or a target from the `lint` aggregate, a
# Dockerfile that stops installing the tool the recipe execs (every gate runs in
# the dev image since #399), a composite action or dependabot config under
# .github/ that changes what CI actually executes, or a fetch/patch script under
# scripts/ that feeds the artifacts the gates read. None of those edits show up
# in config/. The root Dockerfiles are owned by glob so the next
# `<Name>.Dockerfile` is covered the day it lands, the same reason config/ is
# owned as a directory; the explicit lines above are kept because
# tests/bats/agent_docs_codeowners.bats pins each one by name.
/Makefile @Kravalg
/Dockerfile @Kravalg
/*.Dockerfile @Kravalg
/src/test/load/Dockerfile @Kravalg
/.github/ @Kravalg
/scripts/ @Kravalg
